  "title": "CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek, is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nBig Tech leadership is shifting. The Magnificent 7 and Broadcom weakened in June, while the broader market held up better than expected and Micron surged.\nInvestors should respect both the opportunity and the risk. Micron’s growth, margins, and role in AI memory are impressive, but its ra",
  "passage": "…\nA graphics processor (like those designed by Nvidia) powers the AI data center, but memory is just as important. Large AI models need to move huge amounts of data quickly. If data doesn’t reach the processor fast enough, it has to wait, which can be costly.\nHigh-bandwidth memory (HBM) sits close to the AI accelerator and feeds it data at high speed. Storage also matters because training, checkpointing, data retrieval, and inference all require huge amounts of fast, reliable memory and storage infrastructure.\nThat is why Nvidia’s success is spilling over into Micron. Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars. Micron is growing faster right now and, at least in the latest quarter, is showing even fatter margins.\nThat is why investors are watching closely.\nThe broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale. Memory is one of those essential pieces of plumbing.\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners.…",
