  "article_opening": "Accelerate innovation across the DSX ecosystem and bring AI factory solutions to market faster with the open foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nBy Ekin Karabulut and Sam Heywood\nNVIDIA is releasing the full NVIDIA Run:ai platform as open source software, expanding the NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform with a foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nThe release includes four modular components that can be adopted independently: KAI Scheduler for GPU-aware Kubernetes schedulin",
  "passage": "Accelerate innovation across the DSX ecosystem and bring AI factory solutions to market faster with the open foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nBy Ekin Karabulut and Sam Heywood\nNVIDIA is releasing the full NVIDIA Run:ai platform as open source software, expanding the NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform with a foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nThe release includes four modular components that can be adopted independently: KAI Scheduler for GPU-aware Kubernetes scheduling, GPU Fractioning Operator for CUDA-native GPU sharing, KAI Resource Management for organizational quota and hierarchy, and Workload Map for a consistent workload-management contract.\nEight NVIDIA DSX ecosystem partners including Crusoe, Mirantis, Nebius, OpenNebula Systems, Rafay, Saturn Cloud, Spectro Cloud, and vCluster are integrating the open source platform into their offerings.\nNVIDIA is making the full NVIDIA Run:ai platform available as open source software, giving customers, developers, and ecosystem partners access to production-grade AI workload and GPU orchestration capabilities. This release expands the NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform by establishing NVIDIA Run:ai as an open foundation that enables the broader ecosystem to bring differentiated solutions to market faster in response to evolving customer needs.\nOver the past several years, NVIDIA Run:ai has helped organizations run AI workloads on Kubernetes at scale by dynamically allocating GPU resources and managing workloads across the full lifecycle, from data processing and training to inference.\nBy ope…",
