 {
  "id": "G288",
  "article_id": "gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because",
  "passage": "…a. The sell-off follows massive run-ups. Samsung Electro-Mechanics had gained more than 600 percent this year, and Kingboard Laminates more than 470 percent.\nScrapped designs give competitors an opening\nSemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead. The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\nA key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\nA Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg . It shows that Nvidia's most ambitious system is taking longer than expected. The current stock weakness is mostly driven by profit-taking. Shawn Oh of NH Investment & Securities pointed to growing uncertainty around Nvidia's expansion plans, which gives alternative …",
  "statement": "A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman."
 },
 {
  "id": "G266",
  "article_id": "gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57",
  "title": "NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt ",
  "passage": "…pital-constrained AI factories, and DSX ensures the resulting factories run at the token-per-watt efficiency that makes both the operator’s business and NVIDIA’s royalty viable.\nSharon AI and Firmus are the template. Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers. That is an operator adopting NVIDIA’s revenue-per-gigawatt engine and NVIDIA’s financing in the same deal.\nThe $100 Billion Gigawatt Makes Efficiency the Whole Game\nThe economics explain why NVIDIA is willing to give the operating software away. Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade. When a gigawatt costs as much as $100 billion to build, an idle or inefficient watt is not a technical footnote but destroyed capital. In that environment, revenue per gigawatt is the metric that decides whether a $100 billion facility earns its cost of capital, and tokens per megawatt is the lever. DSX is NVIDIA’s claim on that lever. The implication is that the reference design and operating system are not a giveaway but the highest-leverage point in the entire buildout: the software that governs whether the most expensive industrial assets ever built run at a profit.\nWho Captures the Revenue per Gigawatt Is the Open Question\nThe bull case is a flywheel: DSX raises revenue per gigawatt, …",
  "statement": "Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade"
 },
 {
  "id": "G067",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0",
  "title": "Nvidia acts as backstop for customer GPUs in return for cut of cloud revenue",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said:",
  "passage": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\"\nThis is not the first time Nvidia has signed deals like this, having previously done so wi…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G263",
  "article_id": "gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57",
  "title": "NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt ",
  "passage": "…roaching $100 billion per gigawatt make tokens-per-megawatt efficiency the decisive variable\nThe bull case of a compounding efficiency-plus-financing flywheel against the bear case of product-market mismatch and an unresolved split of the upside.\nThe News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\nAnalyst Take: NVIDIA’s revenue-sharing model answered how the company gets paid twice on the same silicon. The DSX reference design and operating system is NVIDIA’s mechanism for raising the revenue per gigawatt that a fixed block of power can produce, and it is the half of the business model that determines whether the recurring cut NVIDIA now takes on cloud revenue is a rich stream or a rounding error. Introduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia"
 },
 {
  "id": "G380",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…ling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) shares actually rose more than 1% on Monday — helped by the company’s denial and a Goldman Sachs note calling the valuation compelling — leaving its market cap around $4.7 trillion. Overseas, PCB suppliers to NVIDIA came under pressure, with reports of sharp intraday declines in names such as Japan’s Ibiden and Hong Kong’s Kingboard Laminates Holdings.\nKyber represents NVIDIA’s shift from selling chips to selling complete AI infrastructure. A single Kyber NVL144 cabinet houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs operating as one unified computing platform, targeting hyperscalers like Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon. A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis.\nThe reported culprit is a component most retail investors have not heard of: the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA also calls the orthogonal backplane), a printed circuit board with up to 78 layers that connects electronic modules within the rack. SemiAnalysis said it “remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.” Stacking 78 layers with signal integrity, thermal tolerance, and yield at volume is a hard engineering problem, and SemiAnalysis frames it as the gating item for NVIDIA’s most ambitious system.\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits. Data Center revenue reach…",
  "statement": "A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis."
 },
 {
  "id": "G197",
  "article_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd",
  "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams ",
  "passage": "…tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\nTurn your Software & Technology expertise into content.\nWhat infrastructure planners should account for\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the…",
  "statement": "Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation."
 },
 {
  "id": "G523",
  "article_id": "gnews:b3dd2a0f914f65ea3ebf2914",
  "title": "NVIDIA Run:ai Is Now Open Source as Part of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform | NVIDIA Technical Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-13",
  "article_opening": "Accelerate innovation across the DSX ecosystem and bring AI factory solutions to market faster with the open foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nBy Ekin Karabulut and Sam Heywood\nNVIDIA is releasing the full NVIDIA Run:ai platform as open source software, expanding the NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform with a foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nThe release includes four modular components that can be adopted independently: KAI Scheduler for GPU-aware Kubernetes schedulin",
  "passage": "…in vLLM and SGLang , where it can be enabled with the --load-format runai_streamer flag, as well as in ModelExpress . Support covers local filesystems, S3-compatible stores, and network file shares alongside several cloud backends detailed below.\nMajor cloud providers now document Model Streamer in their own guidance.\nAWS : Model Streamer ships in the vLLM Deep Learning Container and is the recommended fast path in the official EKS documentation for running AI/ML inference , with tuned concurrency and chunk sizing for S3 .\nGoogle Cloud : Native Cloud Storage support built in collaboration with NVIDIA , including GKE documentation for streaming straight from a bucket into accelerator memory.\nMicrosoft Azure : Azure Blob Storage integration, jointly developed by Microsoft and NVIDIA Run:ai to provide first-class model streaming for Azure customers. Published benchmarks on eight NVIDIA H100 VMs shows 3x-6x faster loading than the default loader and a steady 80 Gbps, compared with approximately 40 Gbps for the default path.\nSeveral AI data infrastructure partners have independently benchmarked Model Streamer and published their results:\nWEKA loaded into GPU memory in just 3.5 seconds, roughly 40% faster than loading from local NVMe—enabling organizations to build petabyte-scale shared model repositories that accelerate model readiness without the time, effort, and capacity required to maintain duplicate model copies across the inference environment.\nVAST Data measured 1.7 secon…",
  "statement": "Microsoft Azure : Azure Blob Storage integration, jointly developed by Microsoft and NVIDIA Run:ai"
 },
 {
  "id": "G374",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…MD ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has the clearest window. Shares are up 157.77% year to date, and Goldman Sachs raised its AMD price target to $640 from $450 on July 6. CEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs. Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion. Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion. A Kyber slip strengthens the in-house silicon case.\nNVIDIA has not confirmed the delay. The affected product is a 2028 item; Blackwell and current Rubin systems remain in short supply. NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysis flags NVDA as 45% undervalued relative to GF Value at current prices. Hyperscalers cannot swap vendors overnight.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth…",
  "statement": "2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion"
 },
 {
  "id": "G312",
  "article_id": "gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250",
  "title": "Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in the last 6 months: 4%\n$NVDA Share Price as of Jul. 2: $195\n52-Week High: $237\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nNvidia ( NVDA ) stock barely moved on Monday after news broke that one of the company’s biggest upcoming products is running late. Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips int",
  "passage": "…holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis.\nA larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\nNvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.\nCloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market. Nvidia has not responded to requests for comment.\nDespite this news, Nvidia stock isn’t showing much reaction, trading close to flat. That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\nSemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\nNvidia’s recent earnings back up why investors aren’t panicking.\nIn the most recent quarter, the company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, with data center revenue alone hitting $75 billion.\nFree ca…",
  "statement": "Nvidia has not responded to requests for comment."
 },
 {
  "id": "G408",
  "article_id": "gnews:1c3c0c58ed5ec3da43292f9b",
  "title": "Jim Cramer Was Left Perplexed By NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA)’s Shares",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-11",
  "article_opening": "We recently published Jim Cramer Insisted Recent AI Chip Selloff Wasn't A Bottom & Discussed These 17 Stocks . NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is one of the stocks discussed by Jim Cramer.\nAI giant NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is currently in the news these days due to reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips. The shares are up by 25% over the past year and by 8% year-to-date. Bank of America reiterated a Buy rating and a $350 share price target on the firm on July 8th. NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:N",
  "passage": "We recently published Jim Cramer Insisted Recent AI Chip Selloff Wasn't A Bottom & Discussed These 17 Stocks . NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is one of the stocks discussed by Jim Cramer.\nAI giant NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is currently in the news these days due to reports of a delay to its next generation AI chips. The shares are up by 25% over the past year and by 8% year-to-date. Bank of America reiterated a Buy rating and a $350 share price target on the firm on July 8th. NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) has denied reports that the Kyber platform is being delayed. Cramer, who has continued to support the firm even though its shares have remained lackluster in 2026, believes that the multiple is too low:\n\"I haven't heard you say that NVIDIA is the great source of funds. . .this Kyber delay, what's changed, what it means, what happened. You know Semi Analysis does pretty good work, saying that everything could be late for NVIDIA because one particular part of their business is late. And, this is I think, eventually [inaudible] filters out, going to, I think, incorrectly hit NVIDIA because I can't even prove the story. But Carl, there are so many long knives out for…",
  "statement": "Bank of America reiterated a Buy rating and a $350 share price target on the firm on July 8th"
 },
 {
  "id": "G338",
  "article_id": "gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08",
  "title": "Maximize Spectral Efficiency with AI-Native RAN and NVIDIA AI Aerial",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "By Cindy Goh\nNVIDIA AI Aerial enables an algorithms-first approach that shifts RAN design from fitting CPU compute budgets to maximizing spectral efficiency through AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models.\nGPU acceleration unlocks higher-order spatial multiplexing, with ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop li",
  "passage": "…ing delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop link adaptation at the cell edge while maintaining latency below the 30-microsecond RAN budget.\nAcademic research validates these gains, including a 20% spectral efficiency improvement from pilotless spatial multiplexing and up to 70% throughput increase for practical DRL link adaptation in high-mobility conditions.\nThe platform supports integrated sensing and communications workloads and enables operators to monetize off-peak GPU capacity by hosting edge AI inference applications.\nNVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\nExplore the NVIDIA AI Aerial platform to build, simulate, and deploy AI-native networks from research to commercial deployment.\nLearn more about commercial Aerial RAN Computer (ARC)-Pro with NVIDIA RTX Pro 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPU.\nSee how Nokia is enabling AI-native 5G-Advanced and 6G networks.\nSpectrum is one of the most valuable assets in wireless communications. Over the last 30 years, telecom operators in the US have spent more than $240B to acquire wireless spectrum . A goal of a radio access network (RAN) system is to extract the maximum spectral efficiency (bits/second/Hertz) possible, which translates into more capacity, stronger network resilience with fewer dropped pack…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution"
 },
 {
  "id": "G537",
  "article_id": "gnews:923baa0debc108433ed2d7e1",
  "title": "OpenAI, Anthropic, and Nvidia had record lobbying quarters",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-22",
  "article_opening": "OpenAI, Anthropic, and Nvidia had record lobbying quarters\nCollectively, they spent nearly $6 million around cybersecurity, child safety, and more.\nis a senior policy reporter at The Verge, covering the intersection of Silicon Valley and Capitol Hill. She spent 5 years covering tech policy at CNBC, writing about antitrust, privacy, and content moderation reform.\nIt was a busy summer for AI lobbyists. Anthropic, OpenAI, and Nvidia each had their highest-spending lobbying quarter between July and ",
  "passage": "…reasingly active in policymaking\nOpenAI’s lobbying looks like a response to some of the fallout from the Hugging Face attack , though the disclosures only list high-level topics. In addition to engaging on issues like copyright, OpenAI lobbied Congress, the White House, the Small Business Administration, and the Commerce and State Departments over cybersecurity evaluations for frontier models, AI safety oversight frameworks, and antitrust protections for information sharing. Nvidia lobbied Congress, as well as several different federal agencies and White House offices, on issues including 6G networks, “responsible AI design,” competition policy, quantum computing, and export control policy.\nApple also had a record-breaking quarter, spending $3.1 million and engaging on issues including intellectual property, tariffs, kids online safety, competition, and domestic semiconductor manufacturing. It engaged on bills that seek to put the onus on app stores to verify users’ ages, for example. It’s the first quarter in which Apple has reported spending more than $3 million on lobbying.\nThe disclosures show that leading AI companies are becoming increasingly active in policymaking, in a quarter that marks a lead-up to the midterm elections. OpenAI didn’t even spend $1 million on lobbying in a single quarter until this year, and it didn’t spend more than half a million until the end of 2024. The most Anthropic spent in a single quarter last year was …",
  "statement": "Apple also had a record-breaking quarter, spending $3.1 million"
 },
 {
  "id": "G494",
  "article_id": "gnews:8ca8a3d62e9d4663c70e7935",
  "title": "Elon Musk Makes Massive Nvidia Move at SpaceX",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-25",
  "article_opening": "Elon Musk is preparing another massive expansion of AI computing capacity, saying xAI's Colossus 2 cluster could more than double its current number of Nvidia chips by year-end. The timetable underscores just how aggressively Musk is scaling AI infrastructure across his businesses and gives Nvidia investors another sign that demand for its newest accelerators remains enormous.\nColossus 2 currently runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s, according to Musk.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s are e",
  "passage": "Elon Musk is preparing another massive expansion of AI computing capacity, saying xAI's Colossus 2 cluster could more than double its current number of Nvidia chips by year-end. The timetable underscores just how aggressively Musk is scaling AI infrastructure across his businesses and gives Nvidia investors another sign that demand for its newest accelerators remains enormous.\nColossus 2 currently runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s, according to Musk.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s are expected to be fully operational next week, followed by another 220,000 in November.\nMusk added that a further 220,000 GB300s could come online by late December if we get lucky.\nThat would bring Colossus 2 to roughly 1.21 million Nvidia GB200 and GB300 chips if every planned tranche arrives on schedule, more than double the 550,000 chips Musk said are currently installed.\nThe buildout is especially notable because xAI is already operating Colossus 1 with 150,000 Nvidia H100s, 50,000 H200s and 30,000 GB200s.\nMusk's companies are racing to secure enough compute for increasingly demanding AI models, putting xAI among the industry's most aggressive infrastructure builders.\nThe expansion also matters for Nvidia because the planned additions are concentrated in its latest-generation GB300 accelerators, highlighting continued demand for its most advanced AI hardware.\nThe …",
  "statement": "Musk added that a further 220,000 GB300s could come online by late December if we get lucky."
 },
 {
  "id": "G181",
  "article_id": "gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e",
  "title": "Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\nJim Cramer doubled down on Nvidia on Monday, urging investors to buy the stock as the chipmaker rejected claims that its next-generation AI rack systems face delays until 2028.\nThe clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year.\nSemiAnalysis Claims Put Nvidia's Kyber Timeline in Doubt\nSemiAnalysis claims the hig",
  "passage": "Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\nJim Cramer doubled down on Nvidia on Monday, urging investors to buy the stock as the chipmaker rejected claims that its next-generation AI rack systems face delays until 2028.\nThe clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year.\nSemiAnalysis Claims Put Nvidia's Kyber Timeline in Doubt\nSemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\nThe firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\nNvidia's supply chain felt the report within hours. Japan's Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest client, fell as much as 10% on Monday, Bloomberg reported.\nKingboard Laminates tumbled 18% in Hong Kong, while Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid 11% in Seoul.\nNvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact. The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\nWhen Blackwell delay reports surfaced in August 2024, Nvidia insisted production would ramp on schedule. It then fixed a design flaw and shipped several billion dollars of Blackwell hardware within months.\nJim Cramer Backs Nvidia Despite the…",
  "statement": "The firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers."
 },
 {
  "id": "G296",
  "article_id": "gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b",
  "title": "Nvidia underperforms peers as report sparks rack delay worries",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Investing.com -- NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) shares ticked up just 0.8% on Monday, starkly trailing a 3.2% surge in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index and lagging rivals Advanced Micro Devices Inc (NASDAQ:AMD), which gained 7.7%, and Broadcom Inc (NASDAQ:AVGO), which jumped 4.4%. The underperformance came as a broad rally for chipmakers collided with a boutique research report alleging significant delays in the AI giant's next-generation server architectures.\nSupply chain research firm Se",
  "passage": "… Samsung Electro-Mechanics Co. slid 8.1%.\nAccording to analysts cited by Bloomberg, these rumored technical setbacks could severely cap the scale-up capacity of Nvidia's future hardware. This perceived vulnerability may create a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms, specifically AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\nMizuho analyst Jordan Klein dismissed such fears in his daily \"Tech Bytes\" note, calling the product delay stories \"more noise.\" \"I would not be worried about NVDA nor looking to sell the stock,\" Klein said.\nThe analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\"\nThe lackluster session keeps the world's most valuable public company squarely rangebound as investors navigate an increasingly jittery AI market. Just last week, erratic headlines regarding Meta Platforms' AI spending ambitions sent tech stocks on a whipsaw ride, underscoring growing market anxiety in the AI trade.\nUltimately, the market's outsized reaction to the unconfirmed supply chain friction reveals a fragile consensus among AI investors after a multi-year, historic run-up. With valuation multiples priced for perfection, even the slightest hint of a product roadmap stumble is enough to m…",
  "statement": "Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand"
 },
 {
  "id": "G499",
  "article_id": "gnews:abe3ac14b6a21b7102c1ba59",
  "title": "Nvidia raises the Shield TV Pro price by $100—seven years after launch - How-To Geek",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-02",
  "article_opening": "By Jon Fingas\nNvidia might have released the Shield TV Pro in 2019, but even that device isn't immune to market pressure. The company has raised the price of the Shield TV Pro to $300, a $100 hike.\nThe company explains the move to 9to5 Google as the result of the technology cost crisis prompted by the AI data center rush. \"The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry,\" Nvidia says in a statement.\nNvidia has simultaneously discontinued the standard Shi",
  "passage": "By Jon Fingas\nNvidia might have released the Shield TV Pro in 2019, but even that device isn't immune to market pressure. The company has raised the price of the Shield TV Pro to $300, a $100 hike.\nThe company explains the move to 9to5 Google as the result of the technology cost crisis prompted by the AI data center rush. \"The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry,\" Nvidia says in a statement.\nNvidia has simultaneously discontinued the standard Shield TV. The Android TV hardware has been sold out for months, but it's now clear no new stock is coming. The firm hasn't said if there will be any future Shield products.\nNot every store is reflecting the Shield TV Pro price increase, so you'll want to buy quickly if you're interested.\nOther brands have already increased their prices. The Apple TV 4K jumped from $129 to $199, while the Google TV Streamer surged from $100 to $150. Even Roku's typically affordable streaming devices are more expensive, starting at $40 (previously $30) for the Streaming Stick .\nIs the Shield TV Pro still a good deal?\nThe Shield TV Pro is old, but its powerful-for-the-time Tegra X1+ chip has kept it relevant for years. …",
  "statement": "Nvidia has simultaneously discontinued the standard Shield TV."
 },
 {
  "id": "G264",
  "article_id": "gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57",
  "title": "NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt ",
  "passage": "…ll case of a compounding efficiency-plus-financing flywheel against the bear case of product-market mismatch and an unresolved split of the upside.\nThe News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\nAnalyst Take: NVIDIA’s revenue-sharing model answered how the company gets paid twice on the same silicon. The DSX reference design and operating system is NVIDIA’s mechanism for raising the revenue per gigawatt that a fixed block of power can produce, and it is the half of the business model that determines whether the recurring cut NVIDIA now takes on cloud revenue is a rich stream or a rounding error. Introduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1 financing model, DSX reframes the gigawatt from a power budget into a revenue function. NVIDIA is now selling the op…",
  "statement": "Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G208",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…re likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids, while 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to accelerate the development and validation of their robots.\nExplore NVIDIA’s open models on Hugging Face .\nExplore genomics and biology research at ICML’s GenBio Workshop on Friday, July 10.",
  "statement": "Humanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids"
 },
