 {
  "id": "G524",
  "article_id": "gnews:cf6df1177e181809c5b6e05f",
  "title": "Nvidia opens up Israeli-founded Run:ai software two years after $800 million acquisition",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-13",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia opens up Israeli-founded Run:ai software two years after $800 million acquisition\nThe chip giant is making the AI workload management platform open source, giving developers and cloud providers access to tools designed to improve the use of costly GPUs.\nNvidia is making the full software platform of Israeli-founded Run:ai available as open source, giving developers, cloud providers and other technology companies access to tools designed to make more efficient use of the expensive graphics",
  "passage": "Nvidia opens up Israeli-founded Run:ai software two years after $800 million acquisition\nThe chip giant is making the AI workload management platform open source, giving developers and cloud providers access to tools designed to improve the use of costly GPUs.\nNvidia is making the full software platform of Israeli-founded Run:ai available as open source, giving developers, cloud providers and other technology companies access to tools designed to make more efficient use of the expensive graphics processing units powering the artificial intelligence boom.\nThe move opens up technology Nvidia acquired in late 2024 for an estimated $800 million, following an eight-month regulatory review in the United States and Europe. Run:ai develops software that helps organizations allocate computing resources across AI workloads, from training models to running them in production. As demand for AI computing has surged, the ability to extract more work from existing hardware has become an increasingly important part of the industry’s infrastructure.\nThe ne…",
  "statement": "Nvidia is making the full software platform of Israeli-founded Run:ai available as open source"
 },
 {
  "id": "G277",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "…scaler orders slow, and insiders have logged 16 recent transactions, net selling.\nNeither thesis has resolved. Blackwell ramps while Trainium and TPU volumes rise in parallel. NVIDIA’s NVLink Fusion strategy lets hyperscalers bolt custom accelerators onto NVIDIA’s fabric so the interconnect stays sticky even when compute does not.\nWatch hyperscaler capex mix, whether networking growth stays vertical, and any China SKU announcement over the next two quarters. Any one breaking hard could tip the call.\nNVIDIA trades at $196 against an analyst consensus target of ~$301.62 as of this writing, implying 53% upside. Coverage skews heavily positive with 10 Strong Buy, 48 Buy, 2 Hold, 1 Sell ratings. Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x.\nPerformance is mixed. NVDA is up 4.59% year to date and 24.06% over the trailing year, but down 12.46% over the past month. The S&P 500 delivered a smaller trailing-year gain, so NVDA outperformed with more turbulence.\nAt $196, NVIDIA remains a buy.\nThe numbers do not argue for selling. A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise. The numbers also do not argue for aggressively adding. Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA’s price list, and management’s NVLink Fusion pivot is an implicit acknowledgment that fighting custom sil…",
  "statement": "Forward P/E sits at 22x, trailing P/E at 29x."
 },
 {
  "id": "G103",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…damage it is causing in Taiwan, a critical manufacturing base for Nvidia that hosts more than 90 percent of global leading-edge chip manufacturing.\nRiding the wave of the global AI frenzy led by generative AI breakthroughs, Nvidia’s AI GPU microchips have made it the world’s most valuable company, and Huang one of the wealthiest people on the planet. For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\nIn the United States and other countries, anger is rising at the prodigious energy and water demands of gargantuan AI data centers. But there is a hidden cost: the toxic supply chain that manufactures advanced semiconductors that fill those data centers. Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled , and the trend is likely to continue as the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of t…",
  "statement": "Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled"
 },
 {
  "id": "G395",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…ip development project remains at an early stage. Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\nDeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months. The hiring process has been conducted discreetly, without publicly posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.\nDespite becoming one of the symbols of China's AI resurgence, DeepSeek has maintained a low profile and has not responded to Reuters' request for confirmation regarding the project.\nGlobally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\nFor DeepSeek, chip development carries greater strategic importance because US export restrictions have prevented Chinese companies from accessing Nvidia's most advanced AI chips.\nThe Chinese government has also continued to encourage domestic technology companies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for…",
  "statement": "OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom"
 },
 {
  "id": "G007",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout",
  "title": "NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑s",
  "passage": "… a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated…",
  "statement": "The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G117",
  "article_id": "gnews:1740f3733eedc34d8bac9b65",
  "title": "MicroLED manufacturer nabs Nvidia director to drive AI data center push",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "By Ben Wodecki\nAMS-Osram, an Austrian semiconductor manufacturer of light and sensor solutions, poached a networking director from Nvidia.\nAshkan Seyedi had served as Nvidia’s lead for technical product marketing on its co-packaged and pluggable interconnect solutions since 2021. In a LinkedIn post , Sayedi revealed he’s taking on the role of VP and GM of AMS-Osram’s optical interconnect business line.\nThe firm, whose headquarters is located on the grounds of an old Austrian castle in Premstätte",
  "passage": "…served as Nvidia’s lead for technical product marketing on its co-packaged and pluggable interconnect solutions since 2021. In a LinkedIn post , Sayedi revealed he’s taking on the role of VP and GM of AMS-Osram’s optical interconnect business line.\nThe firm, whose headquarters is located on the grounds of an old Austrian castle in Premstätten and just a short drive from the city of Graz, is looking to push into the data center space. Its core microLEDs and vertical-cavity surface-emitting laser (VCSEL) technologies are set to power photonic optical connections for AI data centers.\nAMS-Osram is initially targeting its photonic tech for short-distance connections between server racks, with it having already secured an agreement with an unnamed “leading AI data center infrastructure partner.”\nSeyedi said it was “an exciting time” for the photonics manufacturer as it pushes into the AI data center space, stating: “Together, we’ll continue building on the company’s strong digital photonics strategy, bringing cutting-edge micro-emitter solutions to life for the rapidly evolving AI datacom market.”\nAMS-Osram is Europe’s only volume manufacturer of microLEDs. Where Leyard Europe is the continent’s leading consumer display microLED maker, AMS-Osram largely focuses on automotive lighting.\nIts push into the data center space has seen it de-prioritize other segments, notably through the sale of its non‑optical analog and mixed-signal sensor business to Infineon in a deal worth $652 milli…",
  "statement": "having already secured an agreement with an unnamed “leading AI data center infrastructure partner.”"
 },
 {
  "id": "G258",
